WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.430
When you run a basic C program on a Linux
machine, it operates entirely inside user

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.510
mode, or Ring 3. This is an isolated
environment where the program is completely

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.170
powerless. On its own, it cannot touch the
disk, it cannot read the network, and it

4
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.660
cannot write to the screen.

5
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.430
For a program to do anything useful, like
print a simple greeting, it has to reach

6
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.490
the physical hardware. But the operating
system's kernel sits below this boundary in

7
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.480
Ring 0, acting as a strict border guard.

8
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.350
If a program attempts to bypass this
privilege boundary and access the hardware

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.750
directly, the processor raises an exception,
and the kernel immediately terminates

10
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.950
the process. Because direct access is
physically blocked, the program must securely

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.860
ask the kernel to perform
the action on its behalf.

12
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.220
That request is a system call.

13
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.390
When your program uses the C write
function, it is actually calling a wrapper

14
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.620
provided by the standard C library, glibc.

15
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.850
Underneath that wrapper, it has to follow
a rigid hardware convention to cross the

16
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.990
boundary. We can see the truth of this
process by looking at the exact assembly

17
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.220
instructions the program uses
to prepare for the crossing.

18
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.710
This specific sequence of instructions is
the non-negotiable protocol required to

19
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.660
safely pierce the operating
system's privilege boundary.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.190
Before crossing, the glibc wrapper must
pack the details of the request into

21
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.740
specific CPU registers, following
the strict Linux x86-64 convention.

22
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.400
First, the call number goes
into the RAX register.

23
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.400
Here, the wrapper uses the number one
to identify the right system call.

24
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.160
The remaining arguments are then
placed into RDI, RSI, and RDX.

25
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.350
Notice a critical security feature.

26
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.480
The text itself does not
travel inside the registers.

27
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.960
The RSI register only carries the address
of where the text lives in memory.

28
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.100
The trigger moment arrives when the
program executes the syscall instruction.

29
00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.300
This forces the CPU to instantly switch
to kernel mode, elevating to ring 0.

30
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.190
Execution jumps to a fixed predefined
address that the kernel configured at system

31
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:11.430
boot, entry_SYSCALL_64. The user program
has no control over this landing zone.

32
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.280
It cannot choose where it jumps.

33
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:18.100
Once below the boundary, the kernel
takes complete control of the request.

34
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.570
Remember, the kernel is the
core of the operating system.

35
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:27.980
It is not a process. The kernel reads
the RAX register and uses it as an index.

36
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.790
That value of one points to a specific
row in the syscall table, triggering the

37
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.000
correct handler, ksys_write.

38
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.060
The kernel does not trust
the request blindly.

39
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.360
The handler first validates the
file descriptor and the arguments.

40
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.010
Next, the kernel uses a function called
copy_from_user to safely pull the actual text

41
00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.120
from the memory address provided
by the untrusted program.

42
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.930
By forcing the program to use a rigid
table and fetching the memory itself, the

43
00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.700
kernel guarantees it cannot be
hijacked by malicious user code.

44
00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.920
Finally, the device driver
prints the text to the screen.

45
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.730
Once the hardware task is complete, the
journey must finish by returning control to

46
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.450
the user program. The processor triggers
the sysret instruction, dropping the CPU

47
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:16.040
privileges and returning execution
across the boundary back to user mode.

48
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.690
The final numeric result of the system
call operation is carried back in the RAX

49
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:25.310
register. But how does the program know if
the kernel encountered an issue, like a

50
00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:31.330
bad file descriptor? If RAX comes back
with a value between minus 4095 and minus

51
00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.760
one, the glibc wrapper recognizes
this range as an error.

52
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.050
The wrapper simplifies
this for the programmer.

53
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.890
It converts the return value to exactly
minus one and securely stores the real error

54
00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.120
code in a variable called errno.

55
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.290
Because every system call is a complex
trip across a guarded border, failures can

56
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:56.330
happen. This is why the course programs
wrap every call in an exit_if statement and

57
00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:00.600
use perror to translate errno
into a readable message.

58
00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:06.570
Let's review. Which CPU instruction
switches execution from user mode to kernel

59
00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:07.360
mode?

60
00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:16.330
The syscall instruction. Does the RSI
register carry the text to be printed or its

61
00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:17.460
memory address?

62
00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.170
It carries the memory address.

63
00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:29.030
Now open animation A2.1 in the course
hub and run lab TD1 exercise one.
